  ],
  "specialization": "Construct the causal event and assign the ending by the direction of its fruit. The ordinary factors project to the same word sets across two histories. Under caṅ insert aṭ, use ṇi-lopa, shorten root ā before p and retain the internal reduplication. Compare the original or substituted s under 8.3.59 only after ti is present. Projection gives the two written caṅ word sets, preserving the two lexical inputs.",
  "ordered_factors": {
    "common": [
      "6.1.45 ai to ā before non-śit ṇic",
      "7.3.36 puk belongs to the preceding aṅga",
      "styāp + ṇic yields styāpay, with no preceding i before the radical s"
    ],
    "cang": [
      "3.1.48 caṅ replaces cli following the ṇyanta in kartari luṅ",
      "6.4.51 ṇi-lopa, with puk previously attributed to the root side",
      "7.4.1 shortens penultimate ā before p; 6.1.11 supplies the root copy",
      "the stop of the initial cluster survives in the copy under 7.4.61; san-like treatment under 7.4.93 yields ti",
      "the copy vowel immediately precedes a consonant cluster and is heavy by 1.4.11; the laghu-copy condition of 7.4.94 does not lengthen it",
      "8.3.59 and 8.4.41 apply according to the retained teaching sound; 6.4.71 supplies independent initial aṭ"
    ],
    "perfect": "Primary styāpay + ām, followed by kṛ in the selected request pada or bhū/as in P. The ām boundary has the PG95 alternatives.",
    "blessing": "P loses ṇic before yāsuṭ and retains styāp. The ordinary A iṭ route keeps ay; at 2pl the written dh/ḍh alternatives of 8.3.79 are recorded separately."
  },
  "commentarial_anchor": {
